back. And so we've got a strong economy with
growing revenues. We have reduction in the amount
of debt that we have outstanding because of our
limited use of debt to finance the budget, and
we've been able to reduce our cost of the debt that
we have outstanding by lowering the interest rate
through refinancings. So we have the wind at our
back, and our balance sheet is very well positioned
currently.

With respect to refundings, over the last --
over the calendar year 2019, so this calendar year
ending, we've executed transactions totaling
$1.7 billion which saves the state $260 million in
avoided interest costs. And to also provide a
longer term context around that, is we've been able
to, over the last nine years, execute refinancings
totaling $16.2 billion, saving the state
$3.2 billion in avoided interest costs.

So we've been able to take advantage over --
of historically very favorable interest rates and
market conditions to save the state money and to
reduce our long-term fixed costs associated with
debt.

With respect to our debt ratio, our policy

ratio established by the Legislature says: What is

