EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WATKINS: Right. So first,
the reserves. We finished the year 6/30/2019 at
approximately $3.4 billion in general fund
reserves. So that's unspent general revenue, plus
the budget stabilization fund. That is
10.4 percent of general revenues, and that is
adequate -- considered adequate and strong by the
rating agencies.

If I had -- there are a lot of factors that go
into a rating, but if I had to pinpoint a number,
it would be ten percent. So we're right at where
we need to be.

We expect to end 2020, so the current fiscal
year that we're in, we expect to end at three
billion or approximately nine percent. So we're
within shouting distance with no adjustments. And
there are positive adjustments to that number at
the end of the year for unspent budgeted dollars.
So I would expect that number to go up. So I would
say we're where we need to be.

The critical piece, obviously, is going to be:
How much unspent GR does the Legislature leave on
the table in formulating the current spending
plan? The Governor's budget rec. sets the right

mark at a combined general fund reserves of $3.1

C & N REPORTERS 850-697-8314

