13

billion, so a hundred-million-dollar increase
based on the Governor's recommendation to the
Legislature of the spending plan funding the
priorities that he believes are important to the
state.

So that's the bottom line. That's the -- you
know, at the end of the day when you look at
revenues coming in and expenditures going out, that
is the bottom-line measure that the rating agencies
look to.

Given where we are, we've been very, very
fortunate to have the strength of those reserves
to deal with unexpected financial contingencies,
primarily in the recent past, hurricane costs and
being able to front those monies and do the
recovery in anticipation of FEMA's reimbursement.
If we did not maintain adequate liquidity, if we
did not maintain adequate reserves, we would not
have the luxury of that financial flexibility.

But it also serves as a cushion against the
inevitable economic cycles which will occur. For
the last ten years, since the credit crisis, it's
all been in one direction, right? It's all been
up; it's all been good.

And I'm here to say that even a stopped clock

